From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 23:47:45 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(control): press at the drag anchor, not at the click

Windows Terminal hands the core selection *boundaries*, not the cells under
the pointer, and the two ends arrive at different times. The far end comes from
SetEndSelectionPoint, already rounded to the nearest cell edge. The anchor
comes from SetSelectionAnchor on the first movement past the drag threshold -
the touchdown point floored, and one cell further right when the drag runs
leftwards, which is what keeps the character you started on selected.

The press was going out before either of those, at LeftClickOnTerminal, which
carries a *guess* at the anchor: the click position rounded to the nearest
edge. ghostty latches its anchor when the button goes down and will not
re-latch, so the guess stuck - and it is wrong whenever the click lands in the
right half of a character, which is the "selection starts one character late"
report.

So a single click no longer presses at all. All it owes ghostty is that the old
selection goes away, and clear_selection does that without latching anything.
The press moves to SetSelectionAnchor, where the direction is known, preceded
by clear_selection because a press within a cell width of the last one is a
double click to ghostty and would select by word.

Multi-clicks press once per click instead of relying on ghostty's own timing to
agree with Windows'. Because click one always clears the sequence, the replay
starts from zero and two presses mean word, three mean line - deterministically,
where before an ordinary drag could select by word if the timing fell wrong.

Positions now land a quarter of the way into the boundary cell rather than at
its centre. Both are below ghostty's 60%-of-a-cell threshold - the far end of a
drag was never the bug - but 0.1 of a cell is not a margin worth relying on
with a content scale and whole-pixel truncation underneath. ClearSelection uses
the new action too, replacing the press-and-release imitation.

Measured on both sides before changing anything: docs/selection-geometry.md.
Covered end to end by scripts/smoke-harness.ps1, which drags in pixels through
WT's own rounding into the real engine and demands the characters cascadia
would have selected - including the three-presses-inside-one-character case
that has to come back identical.

WT suites: terminalCore 54/54, unitSettingsModel 159/159, unitControl 54/54,
terminalApp 51/51.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01BgDGqEqRBB8L5Ueoq1uuxy
---
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp    | 107 ++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 74 insertions(+), 33 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
index 96ece4277..decd3bea6 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
@@ -820,9 +820,25 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
 
 #pragma region Selection
 
-    // WT hands out selection endpoints in cells; ghostty takes mouse positions
-    // in pixels, relative to the surface. The viewport is the shared frame of
-    // reference - both sides mean "row 0 is the top of what is on screen".
+    // What WT hands out is not a cell under the pointer - it is a selection
+    // *boundary*, already rounded. ControlInteractivity does that rounding
+    // before the core is called: the drag anchor floors the touchdown point and
+    // adds one when the drag runs leftwards, and the far end rounds to the
+    // nearest cell edge. Measured against the real cascadia core in
+    // UnitTests_Control::SelectionDragGeometryTests; see docs/selection-geometry.md.
+    //
+    // ghostty is a boundary model too, but it derives the boundary from where
+    // in the cell the pointer is: at or past 60% of the cell width, the
+    // boundary is the cell's right edge, otherwise its left edge
+    // (SelectionGesture.zig, threshold `@round(cell_width * 0.6)`; confirmed on
+    // the real engine with GHOSTTY_HARNESS_SELECT_SWEEP).
+    //
+    // So to mean boundary B, send a pixel inside cell B below that threshold.
+    // A quarter of the way across leaves margin on both sides: the cell's own
+    // left edge below, the 60% threshold above, with room for the content scale
+    // and for ghostty truncating to whole pixels. The cell *centre*, which this
+    // used to send, also lands below 60% - the far end of a drag was never the
+    // bug - but 0.1 of a cell is not a margin worth relying on.
     void GhosttyControlCore::_mouseTo(const Core::Point cell) const
     {
         if (!_surface)
@@ -838,9 +854,14 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         // Measured, not reasoned: driving this sequence in harness/hwnd-host
         // with GHOSTTY_HARNESS_SELECT on a 200% monitor asked for cells 2..4
         // and got 5..8 back, and asked for 5..9 and got 11..14.
+        //
+        // No padding term: the settings translator pins window-padding-x/y to
+        // zero because WT insets the SwapChainPanel itself. ghostty's own
+        // arithmetic does subtract padding_left before taking the fraction, so
+        // if that ever stops being zero this has to grow the term with it.
         const auto cellWidth = _cellSize.Width / _compositionScale;
         const auto cellHeight = _cellSize.Height / _compositionScale;
-        const auto x = (static_cast<double>(cell.X) + 0.5) * cellWidth;
+        const auto x = (static_cast<double>(cell.X) + 0.25) * cellWidth;
         const auto y = (static_cast<double>(cell.Y) + 0.5) * cellHeight;
         ghostty_surface_mouse_pos(_surface, x, y, GHOSTTY_MODS_NONE);
     }
@@ -863,15 +884,21 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         _mouseDown = false;
     }
 
-    // Windows Terminal calls this *as well as* LeftClickOnTerminal for a single
-    // physical click: the click presses, and then the first drag movement
-    // anchors. Pressing again here made two presses land at nearly the same
-    // place and time, which is a double click to ghostty - so an ordinary drag
-    // selected by word, and only selected by character when the timing happened
-    // to fall outside ghostty's double-click window.
+    // This is where a drag's anchor actually comes from, and it is the only
+    // place that knows it: ControlInteractivity calls it on the first pointer
+    // movement past the drag threshold, with the *touchdown* point floored, and
+    // one cell further right when the drag runs leftwards. That +1 is why the
+    // character you started on stays selected whichever way you drag.
     //
-    // So a press already in flight is left alone and this only moves. The press
-    // belongs to whichever of the two calls arrives first.
+    // The press has to happen here rather than at the click. ghostty latches
+    // its anchor when the button goes down and will not re-latch, so pressing
+    // at click time - before the direction is known - latched WT's *rounded*
+    // click position instead, one character late whenever the click landed in
+    // the right half of a character. That was the reported bug.
+    //
+    // clear_selection first, because a press within one cell width of the last
+    // one is a double click to ghostty and would select by word. The action
+    // ends the click sequence, so this press counts as one.
     void GhosttyControlCore::SetSelectionAnchor(const Core::Point positionCore)
     {
         if (!_surface)
@@ -879,12 +906,8 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
             return;
         }
 
-        if (_mouseDown)
-        {
-            _mouseTo(positionCore);
-            return;
-        }
-
+        _releaseMouseIfHeld();
+        _bindingAction("clear_selection");
         _mouseTo(positionCore);
         ghostty_surface_mouse_button(_surface, GHOSTTY_MOUSE_PRESS, GHOSTTY_MOUSE_LEFT, GHOSTTY_MODS_NONE);
         _mouseDown = true;
@@ -897,23 +920,48 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         _renderNow();
     }
 
-    // The click that begins a selection. ghostty counts its own clicks from the
-    // press events - a double click selects a word, a triple selects a line -
-    // so numberOfClicks is deliberately not forwarded: passing WT's count as
-    // well would double the count and turn a double click into a line select.
+    // The click that begins a selection.
+    //
+    // A single click sends no press at all. The position WT gives here is
+    // rounded to the nearest cell edge, which is a guess at where the selection
+    // should start; the drag anchor that follows knows, because by then the
+    // direction is known. Pressing on that guess is what latched the anchor a
+    // character late. All a single click owes ghostty is that the old selection
+    // goes away, and clear_selection does that without latching anything.
+    //
+    // Multi-clicks do press, once per click, so ghostty's own count reaches the
+    // behaviour Windows already decided on: two presses select a word, three a
+    // line. Replaying rather than forwarding a count keeps the two sides'
+    // click-timing rules out of it - and because click one cleared the sequence,
+    // the replay always starts from zero.
     void GhosttyControlCore::LeftClickOnTerminal(const Core::Point terminalPositionCore,
-                                                 const int32_t /*numberOfClicks*/,
+                                                 const int32_t numberOfClicks,
                                                  const bool /*altEnabled*/,
                                                  const bool /*shiftEnabled*/,
                                                  const bool /*isOnOriginalPosition*/,
                                                  bool& selectionNeedsToBeCopied)
     {
+        if (!_surface)
+        {
+            return;
+        }
+
         // A new physical click ends whatever drag ghostty still thinks is in
-        // progress. Without this the press below is swallowed as a move and the
+        // progress. Without this a press below is swallowed as a move and the
         // click extends the previous selection instead of starting one.
         _releaseMouseIfHeld();
+        _bindingAction("clear_selection");
+
+        if (numberOfClicks > 1)
+        {
+            _mouseTo(terminalPositionCore);
+            for (auto i = 0; i < numberOfClicks; ++i)
+            {
+                ghostty_surface_mouse_button(_surface, GHOSTTY_MOUSE_PRESS, GHOSTTY_MOUSE_LEFT, GHOSTTY_MODS_NONE);
+            }
+            _mouseDown = true;
+        }
 
-        SetSelectionAnchor(terminalPositionCore);
         _renderNow();
         selectionNeedsToBeCopied = CopyOnSelect();
     }
@@ -958,14 +1006,7 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
             return;
         }
         _releaseMouseIfHeld();
-
-        // ghostty has no clear-selection binding. A press and release without
-        // movement is what collapses a selection in its own mouse handling, so
-        // that is what this does. Worth replacing with a real binding upstream:
-        // this is behaviour-by-imitation and would break if ghostty's click
-        // handling ever changed.
-        ghostty_surface_mouse_button(_surface, GHOSTTY_MOUSE_PRESS, GHOSTTY_MOUSE_LEFT, GHOSTTY_MODS_NONE);
-        ghostty_surface_mouse_button(_surface, GHOSTTY_MOUSE_RELEASE, GHOSTTY_MOUSE_LEFT, GHOSTTY_MODS_NONE);
+        _bindingAction("clear_selection");
         _renderNow();
     }
 
